WEBVTT

00:02.461 --> 00:04.715
<i>♪ it seems today
 that all you see ♪</i>

00:04.800 --> 00:07.762
<i>♪ is violence in movies
 and sex on tv ♪</i>

00:07.932 --> 00:11.688
<i>♪ but where are those
 good old-fashioned values ♪</i>

00:11.773 --> 00:14.695
<i>♪ on which we used to rely? ♪</i>

00:14.780 --> 00:18.119
<i>♪ lucky there's a family guy ♪</i>

00:18.204 --> 00:21.293
<i>♪ lucky there's a man who
 positively can do ♪</i>

00:21.420 --> 00:23.005
<i>♪ all the things that make us ♪</i>

00:23.090 --> 00:24.299
<i>♪ laugh and cry ♪</i>

00:24.427 --> 00:30.229
<i>♪ he's... A...
 Fam... Ily... Guy. ♪</i>

00:37.873 --> 00:39.792
I... I don't get it.

00:39.877 --> 00:41.212
You thought this was gonna be

00:41.339 --> 00:43.592
<i>The flea circus from
 coraline, didn't you?</i>

00:43.677 --> 00:46.223
I'm cursed with optimism.

00:50.609 --> 00:52.528
Oh, yous got a good eye.

00:52.614 --> 00:55.033
That there's
a "little ms. Wedgewood" set.

00:55.120 --> 00:57.623
(humming "I'm a little teapot")

00:57.792 --> 01:00.546
<i>♪ this is my handle,
 this is my spout ♪</i>

01:00.673 --> 01:02.634
Well, all the parts seem to be
in the right places.

01:02.719 --> 01:04.513
I'll take it.

01:05.559 --> 01:08.229
Holy crap, a vintage
mr. Potato head

01:08.314 --> 01:10.776
From the good old days
when potatoes were men

01:10.863 --> 01:11.904
And had penises.

01:11.990 --> 01:13.533
Peter, they never had penises.

01:13.618 --> 01:15.787
Well, you could put a nose down
there and it kind of worked.

01:15.874 --> 01:17.750
The nostrils were balls, and...

01:17.836 --> 01:19.086
And so forth.

01:19.214 --> 01:21.508
That's the very potato
I used in medical school

01:21.595 --> 01:24.055
To learn anatomy.
I could let it go for 600.

01:24.142 --> 01:26.060
What are we thinking, lois? Yes?

01:26.229 --> 01:28.064
No, we don't have
that kind of money.

01:28.234 --> 01:30.695
Fine, I'll buy it
with my magic beans.

01:30.823 --> 01:32.324
Those are skittles.

01:32.410 --> 01:35.832
There's no room in this world
for dreamers anymore.

01:39.759 --> 01:41.302
Everything okay, dad?

01:41.387 --> 01:43.557
(sighs) just adult stuff, buddy.

01:43.643 --> 01:46.856
Mom wouldn't let me
buy a toy I wanted.

01:48.403 --> 01:50.322
<i>Man (on tv): Do you own a home,
 but can't afford</i>

01:50.491 --> 01:53.079
<i> The things you need to make
 your life more comfortable?</i>

01:53.164 --> 01:55.166
Yes. Wait, is that...

01:55.293 --> 01:56.836
<i> Hi, I'm tom selleck.</i>

01:56.964 --> 02:00.386
<i>Ask your nurse to turn down your
 breathing machine for a minute</i>

02:00.471 --> 02:02.516
<i> So I can tell you
 how a reverse mortgage</i>

02:02.601 --> 02:04.854
<i> From "hey, alright.
 Reverse mortgages,"</i>

02:04.940 --> 02:06.483
<i> Or "harm," can help you.</i>

02:06.568 --> 02:07.944
This seems like a trick.

02:08.072 --> 02:12.078
<i>I know this seems like a trick,
 but it's not a trick.</i>

02:12.164 --> 02:13.290
Oh, that's nice to hear.

02:13.375 --> 02:14.835
But is this his first rodeo?

02:14.920 --> 02:17.424
<i>Look, this isn't my first rodeo.</i>

02:17.551 --> 02:19.303
<i> And I wouldn't be here
 if I thought</i>

02:19.346 --> 02:21.516
<i> Reverse mortgages
 would hurt anyone.</i>

02:21.643 --> 02:23.896
Well, I certainly believe
magnum p.I.,

02:23.982 --> 02:25.984
But it's easy to say this
stuff fully standing up.

02:26.069 --> 02:29.158
I wonder if he can half-lean
on a fence post and say it?

02:30.413 --> 02:33.417
<i>-(gasps)
 -do you really
 want your adult kids</i>

02:33.544 --> 02:36.006
<i> Fighting over your money
 after you're gone?</i>

02:36.050 --> 02:37.927
<i> Isn't it better
 to spend it all now</i>

02:38.013 --> 02:39.765
<i>So they don't have that option?</i>

02:39.850 --> 02:42.520
He's making good points.
I'll just run this by lois.

02:42.606 --> 02:45.444
<i> Men, please don't run
 things by your wives.</i>

02:45.571 --> 02:49.369
<i>Just go to "www..." I'm kidding.</i>

02:49.455 --> 02:51.791
<i> Here's a phone number
 as big as we can put it.</i>

02:51.919 --> 02:54.171
I'm getting my mr. Potato head.

02:54.256 --> 02:56.376
But that is a lot
of numbers to dial.

02:56.428 --> 02:58.305
<i> Tom: If that's too many
 numbers to dial,</i>

02:58.391 --> 03:01.062
<i> Just say, "siri, I'm old."</i>

03:01.147 --> 03:02.398
Siri, I'm old.

03:02.483 --> 03:04.861
<i> Siri: Dialing
 reverse mortgage company.</i>

03:09.416 --> 03:11.376
Stewie? What is all this stuff?

03:11.461 --> 03:14.800
It turns out rupert only likes
"peppa pig" branded tea sets,

03:14.927 --> 03:16.137
Which honestly, I get.

03:16.222 --> 03:18.892
So I sold my tea set
at twice what I paid,

03:18.978 --> 03:20.187
Then I bought more
stuff to sell.

03:20.314 --> 03:22.275
Really? Was it worth that much?

03:22.402 --> 03:25.240
Oh, sweetie, people have no
clue what antiques are worth,

03:25.325 --> 03:26.743
So you just act like you know.

03:26.829 --> 03:29.458
I actually got a booth at
the flea market next weekend.

03:29.585 --> 03:31.378
I have to say,
I'm weirdly impressed.

03:31.505 --> 03:33.842
Yes, who would've thought
a strange little guy like me

03:33.927 --> 03:37.382
Could get super into
tiny details of old furniture.

03:37.435 --> 03:38.895
Well, if you need
any help, I'm in.

03:38.980 --> 03:40.231
All right.

03:40.317 --> 03:41.609
-Is that...
-Yes, most antiques

03:41.695 --> 03:44.908
Are just racist old packaging
from iconic companies.

03:45.036 --> 03:46.954
Well, mostly syrup companies.

03:47.040 --> 03:48.249
Wow, that's very racist.

03:48.335 --> 03:51.339
Yep. Whole-blurry-shelf racist.

03:55.642 --> 03:57.686
I'm mr. Kiki, the swim teacher

03:57.772 --> 04:00.693
Who doesn't have a pool
and drives very far

04:00.779 --> 04:03.699
To other people's pools
for lessons.

04:05.163 --> 04:07.917
After this lesson,
I sit at panera bread

04:07.961 --> 04:09.045
Until traffic dies down.

04:09.130 --> 04:10.632
<i> Announcer: Panera bread.</i>

04:10.717 --> 04:12.218
<i> Where swim teachers
 from other towns</i>

04:12.304 --> 04:13.596
<i> Wait out traffic.</i>

04:13.682 --> 04:16.310
Oh, my god, is that
the expensive mr. Potato head

04:16.396 --> 04:17.605
From the flea market?

04:17.691 --> 04:19.609
I told you
we couldn't afford that.

04:19.695 --> 04:21.154
Don't worry, I got
a ton of money.

04:21.282 --> 04:23.326
I even bought a jet ski
in lake havasu

04:23.412 --> 04:24.537
In case I ever get out there.

04:24.664 --> 04:26.165
("angel"
by sarah mclachlan playing)

04:26.251 --> 04:28.421
<i>♪ spend all your
 time waiting... ♪</i>

04:28.548 --> 04:31.886
<i> Woman:
 Every year, over 9,000 people</i>

04:32.014 --> 04:35.812
<i> Buy jet skis and then
 leave them at lake havasu.</i>

04:35.939 --> 04:39.946
<i> Last year, over 700
 jet skis were rescued</i>

04:40.032 --> 04:42.827
<i> And sent to some of
 the trashier wisconsin lakes</i>

04:42.871 --> 04:46.419
<i> Where tiny-teeth families
 gave them new lives.</i>

04:46.504 --> 04:50.470
<i> For hundreds of others,
 help came too late.</i>

04:50.555 --> 04:52.432
Where did you get
all this money?

04:52.517 --> 04:54.478
It was easy.
I took out a reverse mortgage,

04:54.606 --> 04:56.816
Which is that thing where
your house gives you free money.

04:56.902 --> 04:59.405
Peter, how could you
be so stupid?

04:59.491 --> 05:02.204
Reverse mortgages
are a total scam.

05:02.289 --> 05:04.416
You need to call
the bank right away.

05:04.503 --> 05:06.505
And take that
mr. Potato head back.

05:06.632 --> 05:09.845
I can't. I wrote "best spuds
4 life" all over the box.

05:09.931 --> 05:12.685
I almost love the box
as much as the guy now.

05:12.771 --> 05:14.773
Peter, we could lose our house!

05:14.900 --> 05:17.863
Relax, lois. Tom selleck
made a whole video about that

05:17.949 --> 05:20.452
For the wives, wearing his
signature short shorts.

05:20.538 --> 05:21.956
<i> Hello, wives. I'm...</i>

05:22.041 --> 05:23.375
Sold.

05:25.966 --> 05:28.512
Now the bank is saying
we could lose the house.

05:28.639 --> 05:30.808
I can't believe I got
duped by tom selleck.

05:30.893 --> 05:32.603
West:
Tom selleck?

05:32.690 --> 05:35.109
Well, there's a name
I haven't heard in years.

05:35.279 --> 05:37.614
Mayor wild west,
you know tom selleck?

05:37.743 --> 05:40.747
Oh, I used to. Many moons ago.

05:40.790 --> 05:41.791
How many?

05:41.877 --> 05:43.462
-Many.
-Wow.

05:43.505 --> 05:46.259
If you fellas would allow me
to jaw at you a minute,

05:46.386 --> 05:47.929
I'll spin you a yarn.

05:48.056 --> 05:50.769
<i> Back when we grew
 younger men's mustaches,</i>

05:50.854 --> 05:53.107
<i> Me and tom carved
 ourselves out a niche</i>

05:53.193 --> 05:57.701
<i> As a hippie country duo
 called "cold hard 'stache."</i>

05:57.828 --> 06:00.499
<i>♪ see a penny, pick it up ♪</i>

06:00.626 --> 06:02.545
<i>♪ all the day
 you'll have to look ♪</i>

06:02.631 --> 06:05.551
<i>♪ for its rightful owner ♪</i>

06:05.720 --> 06:07.347
<i>♪ that's an honorable day ♪</i>

06:07.475 --> 06:11.899
<i>♪ looking for
 its rightful owner. ♪</i>

06:12.945 --> 06:15.114
(audience cheers)

06:16.703 --> 06:19.582
<i> Yeah, we were the talk
 of one corner of town.</i>

06:19.626 --> 06:22.548
<i> But as is often
 the case in these tales,</i>

06:22.591 --> 06:24.468
<i> Drugs entered the picture,</i>

06:24.554 --> 06:26.557
<i> And when that trouble arrived,</i>

06:26.642 --> 06:28.477
<i> Well, let's just say ol' tom</i>

06:28.521 --> 06:30.398
<i>Offered it a place to set down.</i>

06:30.525 --> 06:33.196
You're playing with fire,
kemosabe.

06:34.200 --> 06:35.200
(both grunting)

06:40.088 --> 06:44.304
<i> The next day he wrote a song
 called "walk this way</i>

06:44.389 --> 06:49.941
<i> (even if the crosswalk
 says stop)."</i>

06:50.110 --> 06:51.319
<i> And that's when I knew,</i>

06:51.405 --> 06:53.699
<i>The one-a-day demon had got him.</i>

06:53.784 --> 06:57.583
In the end, one-a-day
was merely a suggestion.

06:57.668 --> 07:00.715
Nearest I can reckon,
he was up to nine or ten a week.

07:00.800 --> 07:04.222
Anyhow, hearing he's peddling
reverse mortgages now

07:04.350 --> 07:05.726
Sounds about right.

07:05.811 --> 07:07.605
Do you know what
a reverse mortgage is?

07:07.690 --> 07:09.735
Nearest I can figure,
it's a loan

07:09.820 --> 07:11.280
That enables the borrower

07:11.366 --> 07:14.536
To access the unencumbered
value of their property.

07:14.623 --> 07:16.625
That sounds kind of slick.

07:16.752 --> 07:19.464
Well, the rub is
if you can't pay it back,

07:19.592 --> 07:21.469
They can snatch your abode.

07:21.596 --> 07:23.975
That strikes me as predatory.

07:24.060 --> 07:25.227
Why would tom do this?

07:25.355 --> 07:26.647
He should know better.

07:26.732 --> 07:29.444
You see, having a mustache
is a sacred oath.

07:29.572 --> 07:31.149
I have a mustache.

07:31.201 --> 07:32.118
I'm proud of you.

07:32.245 --> 07:33.871
He can't do this to people.

07:33.957 --> 07:36.252
I am gonna track him down
and get the money back.

07:36.421 --> 07:37.754
Mr. Mayor,
you're coming with me.

07:37.841 --> 07:40.553
Oh, I'm afraid those
days are behind me.

07:40.680 --> 07:43.467
But I wish you the best of luck.

07:43.561 --> 07:45.104
That's fine, I'll go it alone,

07:45.189 --> 07:47.568
Just like meghan markle
and prince harry.

07:47.653 --> 07:50.491
Sir, your millions from netflix

07:50.577 --> 07:52.370
For no one knows what.

07:52.456 --> 07:53.999
Put it with the rest of them.

07:54.168 --> 07:55.084
(phone dings)

07:55.171 --> 07:57.465
Babe, time to do our daily

07:57.550 --> 08:01.182
$250,000 sponsored
instagram post

08:01.309 --> 08:02.935
For del taco.

08:03.021 --> 08:06.276
Oh, I shouldn't have
left the made-up nonsense.

08:08.909 --> 08:10.953
Well, I'm off to new york city

08:11.039 --> 08:13.083
To find tom selleck
and get our money back.

08:13.168 --> 08:14.961
How do you even know
he's in new york?

08:15.090 --> 08:17.092
'cause he's on
cbs's loudest show,

08:17.177 --> 08:18.845
<i> Blue bloods,
and they film there.</i>

08:18.931 --> 08:20.975
No, peter, you're
not going anywhere.

08:21.061 --> 08:23.313
Even for you,
this is an absurd plan.

08:23.399 --> 08:25.318
Ain't nothin' absurd about this.

08:25.403 --> 08:27.490
-Ma'am.
-Sold.

08:27.575 --> 08:29.577
Mr. Mayor. You came.

08:29.663 --> 08:31.791
That's right.
The only thing that can stop

08:31.876 --> 08:35.048
A bad guy with a mustache
is a good guy with a mustache.

08:35.175 --> 08:38.221
So hop on up,
we're taking a trip.

08:39.518 --> 08:41.813
(neighs)

08:41.898 --> 08:44.110
I can't believe we're
going to new york city.

08:44.195 --> 08:46.447
I've always wanted
to see the twin towers.

08:46.533 --> 08:48.244
(whispers)

08:48.288 --> 08:49.914
(gasps) when?

08:59.395 --> 09:00.855
Look, it's billy joel.

09:00.982 --> 09:03.652
Uhp, no, it's just a drywall guy
dressed up for church.

09:03.738 --> 09:07.494
So, is that whole retail
establishment just m&ms?

09:07.580 --> 09:08.747
Yes, and bad jackets.

09:08.832 --> 09:11.044
Like, so bad that sinbad
wouldn't even wear them.

09:11.129 --> 09:14.009
Not sure I quite
understand new york city.

09:14.094 --> 09:16.180
Oh, well, this
is just tv new york.

09:16.265 --> 09:18.769
You can tell 'cause all the cab
drivers are impossibly white.

09:19.732 --> 09:21.066
I call this place
"the big city,"

09:21.193 --> 09:23.780
So you know
I've been here a while.

09:24.910 --> 09:29.459
Mr. Mayor, allow me
to show you my new york.

09:29.544 --> 09:32.925
Your new york is an espn zone?

09:33.011 --> 09:35.682
I-I don't know,
I like all the screens.

09:36.602 --> 09:38.104
Peter, look at this.

09:39.734 --> 09:41.026
Black hair dye.

09:43.785 --> 09:45.912
We follow this trail,
we find selleck,

09:45.998 --> 09:49.211
Or possibly papa john
or rudy giuliani,

09:49.297 --> 09:52.343
But that's a risk
we're gonna have to take.

09:58.025 --> 10:00.320
Brian, time to
teach you my techniques.

10:00.488 --> 10:01.697
Watch and learn.

10:01.824 --> 10:03.159
You have a good eye.

10:03.244 --> 10:05.664
I just sold a piece
by the same artist for 40.

10:05.750 --> 10:08.003
-(whispers): Lie.
-That's a little
out of my budget.

10:08.088 --> 10:11.177
Well, I'd like 30,
but I'd take 25.

10:11.262 --> 10:13.807
(sucks teeth) lowest I could
let it go for is 20.

10:13.893 --> 10:15.018
Would you take 11?

10:15.103 --> 10:17.023
Well the frame alone
is worth 30.

10:17.067 --> 10:18.442
Highest I can do is 12.

10:18.528 --> 10:19.737
Okay, but you got to pay me

10:19.822 --> 10:21.574
Before my wife gets back
from the bathroom.

10:21.701 --> 10:23.496
She's gonna kill me
over this price.

10:25.168 --> 10:27.963
That's the dance. Give it a try.

10:28.091 --> 10:30.594
Hi, there, uh,
how much for this vase?

10:30.679 --> 10:32.473
Five.

10:32.601 --> 10:33.810
What the hell was that?

10:33.895 --> 10:35.271
That's not the dance, man.

10:35.357 --> 10:37.234
You got to put your hands up
higher, move around a bit.

10:37.361 --> 10:38.737
My dance is the tango.

10:38.823 --> 10:40.282
You got to find yours. Go ahead.

10:40.326 --> 10:42.746
-Stewie, I'm not doing thi...
-Find your dance!

10:42.915 --> 10:44.291
A bolero man.

10:44.419 --> 10:46.587
Little dirty,
but nobody's gonna bleed.

10:46.715 --> 10:49.344
You feel that? That's the bluff.

10:49.430 --> 10:50.596
I thought it was "the dance."

10:50.682 --> 10:51.933
Right, but at some point,

10:52.018 --> 10:53.812
The dance becomes the bluff.

10:53.897 --> 10:55.732
Are you just making
all this up as you go?

10:55.819 --> 10:57.445
That's the dance, bri.

10:58.741 --> 11:00.660
All right, brian,
follow my lead.

11:00.788 --> 11:02.414
"van blunder" time.

11:02.584 --> 11:04.210
Ooh, how much for this?

11:04.296 --> 11:05.004
You're gonna hate me.

11:05.131 --> 11:06.632
So, everything you see here

11:06.759 --> 11:08.595
Is for sale, except that.

11:08.680 --> 11:09.890
I don't even know
how it got out here,

11:09.975 --> 11:11.476
It's supposed to be in the van.

11:11.562 --> 11:13.397
Well, I really like it.

11:13.483 --> 11:15.151
Brian, what did you do?

11:15.320 --> 11:17.907
Oh, boy. That was
a blunder on my part.

11:17.992 --> 11:20.537
That's a... That's a...
That's a pretty big blunder.

11:20.581 --> 11:22.291
But look how much she loves it.

11:22.461 --> 11:23.377
Do you love it?

11:23.463 --> 11:26.175
I do. I can give you $10.

11:26.260 --> 11:28.764
Well, I do want it to go
to someone who loves it.

11:28.891 --> 11:31.479
I'll tell you what,
give me 11, and it's a deal.

11:31.564 --> 11:33.191
What a blunder.

11:33.276 --> 11:37.032
This is awkward, but
y'alls horning in on my turf.

11:37.076 --> 11:40.248
See, "grandma's little loft"
is the king of knick-knacks.

11:40.375 --> 11:41.960
Now yous selling 'em.

11:42.003 --> 11:43.255
Ha. That's a laugh.

11:43.340 --> 11:44.716
We'd never sell knick-knacks.

11:44.843 --> 11:46.094
We're tchotchke guys.

11:46.221 --> 11:48.808
If yous calling that
vintage chatillon dial-face

11:48.852 --> 11:50.478
Hanging merchant's
scale a tchotchke,

11:50.606 --> 11:51.982
Y'alls sicker than I thought.

11:52.109 --> 11:53.610
Says the guy who has
his what's-its

11:53.737 --> 11:55.281
Mixed in with his who's-its.

11:55.408 --> 11:56.867
That's potpourri.

11:56.995 --> 12:00.083
Yous out of your league.
That's what's happening here.

12:00.168 --> 12:01.587
You hearing footsteps, gramps?

12:01.630 --> 12:03.299
Hows about a little contest?

12:03.384 --> 12:05.846
Whoever sells the most
by end of day tomorrow

12:05.973 --> 12:07.391
Is the flea market champ.

12:07.560 --> 12:11.358
The loser gets out
of the game. For good.

12:11.444 --> 12:12.862
Fine, you're on.

12:12.947 --> 12:14.657
And by the way,
there was a dead cricket

12:14.743 --> 12:16.076
In the teapot you sold me.

12:16.204 --> 12:19.083
-Oh, no.
-I know.

12:21.883 --> 12:24.554
The hair dye trail
ends right here.

12:26.769 --> 12:27.853
(doorbell rings)

12:27.938 --> 12:30.817
Wait, only one guy
would accept delivery

12:30.903 --> 12:33.156
Of so many hawaiian shirts.

12:34.787 --> 12:35.995
(snoring)

12:36.082 --> 12:38.084
(mustache barking)

12:38.921 --> 12:41.049
What is it, boy?

12:42.345 --> 12:45.517
Man, I love being paid
for reverse mortgages

12:45.602 --> 12:47.312
In hawaiian shirts.

12:47.356 --> 12:49.359
So, it looks like
there's a few more

12:49.444 --> 12:51.405
Disgruntled customers
down there.

12:53.453 --> 12:56.499
Well, that's a 'stache
from the past.

12:56.626 --> 12:58.629
This can't be good. Get in.

12:59.925 --> 13:01.761
Hey, nypd?

13:01.847 --> 13:04.684
Yeah, it's chief whatever-
my-character's-name-is

13:04.811 --> 13:05.811
<i>From blue bloods.</i>

13:05.939 --> 13:07.481
There's a couple of trespassers

13:07.609 --> 13:08.943
Outside my house.

13:09.070 --> 13:10.447
(police siren wails)

13:11.910 --> 13:14.121
You guys are under arrest.

13:14.249 --> 13:17.003
-Is this 'cause I rubbed
on a lady in the subway?
-No.

13:17.088 --> 13:19.048
-'cause I rubbed on
a lady on the bus?
-No.

13:19.177 --> 13:20.719
Do you do a lot of
rubbing on people?

13:20.847 --> 13:23.476
Hey, eight million people,
we all got to fit somewhere.

13:31.286 --> 13:32.454
(sighs)

13:32.581 --> 13:35.126
I'm sorry for dragging
you into this, mr. Mayor.

13:35.212 --> 13:37.757
Lois was right,
this was a stupid plan.

13:37.842 --> 13:39.427
Don't beat yourself up, hoss.

13:39.513 --> 13:40.847
When your cause is just,

13:40.933 --> 13:42.976
Any action you take
is honorable.

13:43.103 --> 13:46.819
Besides, fate has a funny way
of takin' care of folks.

13:46.904 --> 13:48.572
(snores)

13:53.168 --> 13:58.177
I'd be much obliged if you'd
go ahead and open up for me.

13:58.973 --> 14:00.265
How did you do that?

14:00.392 --> 14:01.810
Well, this ain't
the first schlage

14:01.895 --> 14:04.107
To which I've pitched woo.

14:06.781 --> 14:08.700
Oh, brian, look,

14:08.785 --> 14:10.036
It's this antique's last day

14:10.122 --> 14:11.582
Selling antiques.

14:11.709 --> 14:14.004
You alls can eat my dust.

14:14.089 --> 14:15.381
(coughing)

14:15.466 --> 14:18.138
Oh, we got quite
a showdown here today

14:18.223 --> 14:19.850
At flea market regionals, jim.

14:19.935 --> 14:21.979
"grandma's little loft"
has the experience,

14:22.023 --> 14:25.111
But don't count out "the brian,
the bitch, and the wardrobe."

14:25.238 --> 14:27.993
And the question we've been
hearing the most today:

14:28.078 --> 14:30.331
Why does a flea market
have announcers?

14:30.459 --> 14:32.252
Well, sometimes major
network announcers

14:32.379 --> 14:34.465
Have one too many
at a golf tournament

14:34.635 --> 14:35.760
And hit a guy in their car

14:35.887 --> 14:37.513
And then have to
work their way back.

14:37.599 --> 14:40.604
Hi, do you have any vintage
southwestern lifestyle stuff?

14:40.689 --> 14:42.650
Bro, it's 7:30 a.M.

14:42.777 --> 14:44.445
That stuff is long gone by 5:15.

14:44.531 --> 14:46.742
I's about to open
a storage container

14:46.827 --> 14:48.997
Fresh from tempe. Come on by.

14:49.166 --> 14:50.459
-What?
-How is that possible?

14:50.586 --> 14:52.046
<i> Announcer:
 And in crunch time,</i>

14:52.131 --> 14:53.465
<i> The game just slows down</i>

14:53.592 --> 14:56.389
<i> For a veteran booth
 like "grandma's little loft."</i>

14:56.474 --> 14:58.226
<i> Announcer 2: A large,
 native american-looking teen</i>

14:58.311 --> 15:00.231
<i> Has grumpily plopped down
 on a camp chair</i>

15:00.316 --> 15:02.360
<i> With an ipad at bruce's booth.</i>

15:02.487 --> 15:04.280
This guy's on another level.

15:04.366 --> 15:06.327
-There's no way
we can catch him.
-(rattling)

15:06.454 --> 15:08.541
<i> Announcer:
 This could be
 the nail in the coffin</i>

15:08.626 --> 15:10.670
<i> As other booths have
 started shaking</i>

15:10.797 --> 15:12.925
<i> Their mis-prescribed
 opiates in salute.</i>

15:13.011 --> 15:14.762
<i> Announcer 2: They call that
 the "battle rattle,"</i>

15:14.848 --> 15:17.686
<i> And it's the ultimate
 flea market show of respect.</i>

15:17.813 --> 15:19.773
<i> This one's all but over.</i>

15:22.991 --> 15:25.035
Welcome back to
flea market regionals,

15:25.121 --> 15:26.455
Brought to you by warm coke.

15:26.540 --> 15:29.587
Warm coke:
Sometimes they're just warm.

15:29.672 --> 15:31.215
<i> Announcer 2:
 "grandma's little loft"</i>

15:31.301 --> 15:34.055
<i> Is putting the finishing
 touches on a dominant win.</i>

15:34.140 --> 15:35.266
Hey, we got lots of great

15:35.393 --> 15:37.980
One-of-a-kind
stuff here. Please.

15:38.066 --> 15:40.904
<i> Announcer:
 And, of course, the old guy
 who last-minute decides</i>

15:41.031 --> 15:43.910
<i> He wants to keep his stuff has
 never really been a factor.</i>

15:43.995 --> 15:45.873
<i> Announcer 2:
 For him, it's more
 about telling people</i>

15:46.000 --> 15:47.502
<i> Where he got the stuff.</i>

15:47.629 --> 15:49.965
(sighs) well,
we gave it a good shot,

15:50.051 --> 15:51.510
But we're not gonna catch bruce.

15:51.595 --> 15:53.138
I guess we should go
tell him he won.

15:53.224 --> 15:55.685
<i> Announcer:
 Hold the phone.
 Looks like we got a guy</i>

15:55.814 --> 15:58.651
<i> Bringing his home trash
to the flea market garbage can.</i>

15:58.736 --> 16:00.696
<i> Announcer 2:
 And this is where
 the lone garbage can</i>

16:00.825 --> 16:03.119
<i> Being right by
 "grandma's little loft"</i>

16:03.247 --> 16:04.413
<i> Could come into play.</i>

16:04.541 --> 16:06.000
<i> Announcer:
 Sure looks like a bag</i>

16:06.128 --> 16:08.464
<i> Of mostly full diapers
 and half-eaten burritos.</i>

16:08.591 --> 16:10.845
<i> Announcer 2:
 And that's just
 a bee's dream bag.</i>

16:10.972 --> 16:12.974
Hey, we just wanted
to say, you wo...

16:13.144 --> 16:15.897
-(loud buzzing)
-(people screaming)

16:21.077 --> 16:24.792
<i> Announcer:
 And once that money
 belt is zipped...</i>

16:24.878 --> 16:27.631
It's official. "the brian,
the bitch, and the wardrobe"

16:27.717 --> 16:30.638
Win flea market regionals.
What a comeback.

16:30.724 --> 16:33.519
Boy, you said it.
I told espn I'd quit

16:33.605 --> 16:35.356
After a "magic: The gathering"
tournament,

16:35.442 --> 16:37.862
But this time, I'm serious.

16:37.947 --> 16:40.159
Well, yous won fair and square.

16:40.286 --> 16:42.789
Y'alls the best
flea market weirdos.

16:42.917 --> 16:44.001
(bees buzzing)

16:44.086 --> 16:45.922
I sure am gonna miss this place.

16:46.082 --> 16:47.175
Sorry about the bees.

16:47.344 --> 16:49.931
Part of the game.
Hey, before I go...

16:50.058 --> 16:51.893
Would y'all be willing to
sell your whole inventory

16:51.978 --> 16:53.773
For nine dollars?

16:53.858 --> 16:55.485
-Would you do 11?
-No.

16:55.612 --> 16:57.907
You got yourself a deal.
I never want to come back here.

16:57.992 --> 17:01.080
Everyone, you're all within
500 feet of a school.

17:01.165 --> 17:02.625
(shouting)

17:02.710 --> 17:05.465
<i> Chris:
 Over 200 arrests
 were made that day.</i>

17:05.550 --> 17:07.302
<i> It's still known
 in flea market lore</i>

17:07.388 --> 17:09.306
<i> As "extinction Sunday."</i>

17:09.392 --> 17:11.812
<i> I'm chris griffin,
 and my brother and my dog</i>

17:11.939 --> 17:14.276
<i> Left me in a very hot car.</i>

17:18.036 --> 17:19.830
Hi, shannon, the line producer,

17:19.915 --> 17:21.500
Said I could have another
plate of lasagna.

17:21.585 --> 17:24.089
That's not shannon's call.

17:24.174 --> 17:25.801
You were right.

17:25.886 --> 17:27.138
Becoming extras on his show

17:27.223 --> 17:28.850
Was the only way
to get close to tom.

17:28.935 --> 17:32.232
Once the cameras start rolling,
we'll make our move.

17:32.318 --> 17:34.320
<i>Blue bloods, episode a million,</i>

17:34.406 --> 17:36.283
Season blabbity-blah.
Hollywood, go.

17:36.368 --> 17:37.995
These guys are pros.

17:38.122 --> 17:39.415
Director:
Action.

17:39.584 --> 17:42.421
<i>Chief whatever-your-character's-
name-is from blue bloods,</i>

17:42.506 --> 17:44.301
Do you recognize this man?

17:44.386 --> 17:46.890
I do. He owns
the bodega on 56...

17:46.975 --> 17:47.892
<i> Blue bloods.</i>

17:47.977 --> 17:50.063
-Director: Cut.
-Who is this guy?

17:50.149 --> 17:51.682
My name is peter griffin,

17:51.735 --> 17:53.362
And I'm here 'cause
you screwed me over

17:53.490 --> 17:55.158
With your trick
reverse mortgage.

17:55.202 --> 17:56.577
It's not a trick.

17:56.662 --> 17:58.122
Overruled.

17:58.249 --> 18:00.252
What are you... You're an actor.

18:00.380 --> 18:02.549
Time to acknowledge
the corn, tom.

18:02.676 --> 18:06.099
Wild west? I had you arrested.

18:06.184 --> 18:07.811
When you got hooked on multis

18:07.938 --> 18:10.691
And started singing
mean-spirited songs,

18:10.777 --> 18:13.490
I figured you were young,
it was a phase.

18:13.617 --> 18:16.329
But now you went and got mixed
up with reverse mortgages,

18:16.456 --> 18:19.420
And that, partner,
violates the mustache code.

18:19.505 --> 18:22.008
Yeah. And besides,
you're magnum p.I.

18:22.177 --> 18:23.429
You're like my tv boss.

18:23.514 --> 18:25.892
Regular guys like me trust you.

18:25.978 --> 18:28.398
I didn't make you do anything.

18:28.483 --> 18:31.530
Only one thing left to try.

18:33.578 --> 18:36.290
<i>♪ see a penny, pick it up ♪</i>

18:36.375 --> 18:38.420
<i>♪ all the day
 you'll have to look ♪</i>

18:38.505 --> 18:41.342
<i>♪ for its rightful owner ♪</i>

18:41.428 --> 18:43.181
<i>♪ that's an honorable day ♪</i>

18:43.308 --> 18:48.442
<i>♪ looking for
 its rightful owner ♪</i>

18:49.529 --> 18:51.908
I-I'm not gonna sing.

18:51.993 --> 18:54.371
-Your honor?
-I'll allow it.

18:54.499 --> 18:56.585
<i>♪ a penny saved is
 a penny earned ♪</i>

18:56.712 --> 18:59.049
<i>♪ but if it ain't yours,
 I've learned ♪</i>

18:59.134 --> 19:05.229
<i>♪ to find its rightful owner. ♪</i>

19:05.314 --> 19:07.024
(applause)

19:07.152 --> 19:11.493
This court finds that the world
could use more songs like that.

19:11.620 --> 19:14.541
Peter, I think you and I
might just have to

19:14.627 --> 19:16.462
Agree to disagree
when it comes to

19:16.589 --> 19:19.803
The financial propriety
of reverse mortgages.

19:19.930 --> 19:22.183
That said, I certainly
never meant

19:22.310 --> 19:23.853
To cause you any distress.

19:23.981 --> 19:26.317
And wild, back in the day,

19:26.444 --> 19:28.238
I turned my back on you,

19:28.323 --> 19:30.952
And I turned my back
on my mustache.

19:31.079 --> 19:33.541
Think you can forgive me?

19:33.626 --> 19:36.381
Eh, nothin' a firm,
two-handed handshake

19:36.466 --> 19:38.761
With eye contact can't solve.

19:41.269 --> 19:43.521
And peter, I don't want there

19:43.608 --> 19:45.233
To be any hard feelings,

19:45.320 --> 19:46.903
So I'm gonna write you a check

19:47.073 --> 19:49.744
For a little bit of
the lot of money I've made

19:49.871 --> 19:52.249
Selling legitimate
reverse mortgages

19:52.335 --> 19:54.337
To people who,
to the best of my knowledge,

19:54.423 --> 19:56.717
Understand fully what
they're entering into.

19:56.844 --> 19:58.387
How are you 78?

19:58.473 --> 19:59.849
This ought to take care of it.

19:59.934 --> 20:02.271
And hey, just to
make sure we're good,

20:02.357 --> 20:04.902
How'd you like
a line on the show?

20:04.987 --> 20:06.155
<i> Blue bloods.</i>

20:06.240 --> 20:08.952
It might not be just
saying the show.

20:09.038 --> 20:11.541
I understand.
Tom, would it be possible

20:11.627 --> 20:13.087
For me to get another
plate of lasagna?

20:13.214 --> 20:15.676
That's not my call, peter.
That's shannon's call.

20:15.761 --> 20:17.554
It's not shannon's call.

20:25.241 --> 20:26.701
Follow that car.

20:26.828 --> 20:28.328
First time in the big city?

20:28.456 --> 20:29.665
<i> Blue bloods.</i>

20:29.750 --> 20:31.460
That last line was improvised.

20:31.546 --> 20:34.592
Oh, look out, ryan stiles, huh?

20:34.720 --> 20:36.723
Oh, you know, I'm just glad

20:36.808 --> 20:39.060
We don't have to worry about
losing our house anymore.

20:39.147 --> 20:40.940
Me, too. I guess I learned

20:41.025 --> 20:42.736
Celebrities always
do the right thing

20:42.821 --> 20:45.199
Once you publicly
guilt them into it.

20:45.326 --> 20:47.788
♪ ♪